page("about","About Texas Folding Grill","What Texas Folding Grill covers, who publishes it, and where product facts come from.","full01",f"""
<p>Texas Folding Grill is about cooking Texas-style on a small, folding charcoal grill: the wood, the fire, the beef and sausage, and the trips where Texans cook.</p>
<h2>Who publishes it</h2><p>This site is published by Slot Grill. The <a href="{HOME}">Slot Grill</a> is a collapsible carbon steel charcoal grill made in the USA by Keith in Buna, Texas, in a Compact and a Full Size with an optional griddle. Every cook here is written for it.</p>
<h2>How we handle facts</h2>{ul(["Grill specs and prices come from SlotGrill.com, which is always the authority.","Safe temperatures follow USDA minimums.","No invented reviews, ratings or testimonials."])}
<p>Questions? Use the <a href="{CONTACT}">Slot Grill contact form</a>.</p>
""","about")
page("faq","Texas Folding Grill FAQ","Answers about Texas-style cooking on a small folding charcoal grill: brisket, sausage, wood, burn bans, and the Slot Grill.","c07","<p>Every question from across the site, grouped by topic.</p>","about")
page("glossary","Texas BBQ Glossary","Plain definitions of Texas barbecue and folding-grill terms: bark, packer, flat, point, hot links, post oak, two-zone and more.","full10","<dl>"+"".join("<dt><b>%s</b></dt><dd>%s</dd>"%t for t in [
("Bark","The dark, flavorful crust on smoked meat."),("Brisket flat","The leaner, thinner muscle of a brisket; the practical size for a small grill."),("Brisket point","The fattier, thicker muscle; source of burnt ends."),("Packer","A whole brisket, flat and point together."),("Dalmatian rub","Salt and coarse black pepper."),("SPG","Salt, pepper, garlic."),("Hot links","Spicy Texas sausage links."),("Post oak","A Texas oak that's the classic Central Texas barbecue wood."),("Mesquite","A hot-burning South Texas wood with strong flavor."),("Probe-tender","When a thermometer probe slides in with almost no resistance."),("Two-zone fire","Coals on one side, none on the other."),("Banked fire","All coals at one end, for indirect cooking."),("Wrap / Texas crutch","Wrapping meat in foil or paper partway through a cook."),("Rest","Holding cooked meat before slicing so juices settle."),("Fajita","Originally skirt steak; now grilled meat strips with tortillas."),("Griddle (plancha)","A flat steel plate cooked on like a flat top."),("Air gap","On the Slot Grill, the space between the table and the bottom of the coal bed."),("Tab and slot","How the Slot Grill's steel panels lock together by hand.")])+"</dl>","about")
